Letting $x=-1$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-1-2)^n}{n^2\cdot3^n}
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{(-3)^n}{n^2\cdot3^n} \\\\
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{(-1)^n\cdot 3^n}{n^2\cdot3^n} \\\\
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2}
\end{align}$

By the alternating series test, we know this series converges.

Letting $x=5$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(5-2)^n}{n^2\cdot3^n}
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{3^n}{n^2\cdot3^n} \\\\
 &=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{1}{n^2}
\end{align}$

By the $p$-series test, we know this series converges.
